<section style="padding-top:40px">
  <div class="wrap" style="max-width:820px">

    <div class="guide-toc">
      <h4>In this guide</h4>
      <a href="#fairness">Fairness is a rate, not a game count</a>
      <a href="#constraints">The five things a rotation is balancing</a>
      <a href="#queue">Why a queue works - and what it cannot know</a>
      <a href="#late">Late arrivals expose bad fairness rules</a>
      <a href="#balance">When balance should override strict order</a>
      <a href="#variety">Partner variety without turning it into a quota</a>
      <a href="#manual">A practical manual system</a>
      <a href="#software">When software becomes useful</a>
      <a href="#faq">Frequently asked questions</a>
    </div>

    <div class="prose" style="margin-top:34px">
      <p class="lead"><strong>The fairest player is not always the person whose peg is first.</strong> A good rotation gives comparable playing opportunity to people who have been available for comparable amounts of time, while still producing games worth playing.</p>

      <p>That sounds like a small distinction. It changes almost everything: how you treat late arrivals, how you recover from an unusually long game, whether somebody can play twice while another waits, and how much freedom you have to improve a dreadful four-player combination.</p>

      <h2 id="fairness">Fairness is a rate, not a game count</h2>
      <p>Suppose Alice arrives at 7pm and has played four games by 8:30. Ben arrives at 8pm and has played two. They have the same rate of games per hour present. Giving Ben two immediate games merely to make the totals equal would make the night <em>less</em> fair to Alice.</p>

      <p>The useful denominator is <strong>time available to play</strong>.</p>

      <p>You can think about it informally as:</p>
      <p style="text-align:center;font-size:1.05rem"><strong>playing opportunity = court time received ÷ time available</strong></p>

      <p>You do not need to calculate a percentage after every rally. The point is conceptual: compare people against the opportunity they could reasonably have received while they were there.</p>

      <p>This also explains why "everyone gets five games" is often a poor target. People arrive late, leave early, sit out voluntarily, play games of different lengths and sometimes spend ten minutes helping with the nets. Fairness needs context.</p>

      <h2 id="constraints">The five things a rotation is balancing</h2>
      <p>Once the basic opportunity to play is protected, a club normally cares about four other things.</p>

      <ol>
        <li><strong>Waiting:</strong> nobody should repeatedly get jumped while available.</li>
        <li><strong>Game quality:</strong> four players should make a plausible contest.</li>
        <li><strong>Partner variety:</strong> the same pair should not reform by accident all evening.</li>
        <li><strong>Opponent variety:</strong> players should not repeatedly face the same two people.</li>
        <li><strong>Rest and preference:</strong> somebody may want a breather, a specific partner, or a league-practice game.</li>
      </ol>

      <p>The interesting part is that these conflict.</p>

      <p>Strictly taking the next four protects queue order but can make a 21-4 game. Choosing the four closest ratings can repeatedly delay an outlier. Maximising variety can split the one partnership that actually needs practice. Giving every preference priority makes the queue meaningless.</p>

      <p>A rotation system is therefore not a formula for perfection. It is a hierarchy of compromises.</p>

      <h2 id="queue">Why a queue works - and what it cannot know</h2>
      <p>The physical pegboard has lasted for decades for good reasons. It is visible, tangible and self-explanatory. Move your peg when you come off; watch it travel towards the front; play again.</p>

      <p>That transparency creates trust. Nobody needs to know what an algorithm thinks of them.</p>

      <p>But a plain queue only stores one dimension well: <strong>order</strong>. It does not inherently know that:</p>
      <ul>
        <li>the first four would make an enormous mismatch;</li>
        <li>two of them partnered in each of their last three games;</li>
        <li>one player has already had unusually long games and therefore more court time;</li>
        <li>another player has just asked to sit out;</li>
        <li>or the person fifth in line has actually waited much longer because they were skipped for a legitimate reason last round.</li>
      </ul>

      <p>Good clubs compensate with human judgement. The board is simple because the organiser is carrying the complexity.</p>

      <h2 id="late">Late arrivals expose bad fairness rules</h2>
      <p>A late arrival should not jump straight onto court simply because they have played zero games. They were not available while everybody else was playing.</p>

      <p>But "put them at the back" is only correct at the moment they arrive.</p>

      <p>Imagine a player joins at 8pm just as four people go on. They wait through that game. When the court returns, the rest of the room may already have played several times. The newcomer now has a genuine period of waiting and zero court time since arrival. Continuing to hold them behind everybody who happened to sign in earlier can become unfair very quickly.</p>

      <p>The clean rule is:</p>
      <p><strong>give no credit for time before arrival; give full credit for waiting after arrival.</strong></p>

      <p>The same logic handles early leavers, sit-outs and injuries. Count the periods in which a player was actually available.</p>

      <h2 id="balance">When balance should override strict order</h2>
      <p>There is a point at which preserving queue purity creates a bad enough game that nobody benefits.</p>

      <p>If the next four are two strong county players and two beginners, there are at least three possibilities:</p>
      <ul>
        <li>play strong pair versus beginner pair - transparent and dreadful;</li>
        <li>split strong and developing players across the sides - often much better;</li>
        <li>delay one or more players briefly to create two more appropriate games.</li>
      </ul>

      <p>The second option costs nothing in queue fairness because the same four still play. It should usually be the first adjustment.</p>

      <p>The third is where policy matters. A social mixed club may accept a small extra wait to avoid a serious mismatch. A club that promises strict first-four rotation may not. What causes resentment is not either rule - it is silently changing between them depending on who is standing at the board.</p>

      <p>Publish the principle. For example:</p>
      <p><em>We normally take from the front of the queue, but may look a few places ahead to make a competitive game. Anyone passed over gets priority in the next suitable game.</em></p>

      <p>Now the discretion is part of the system rather than favouritism.</p>

      <h2 id="variety">Partner variety without turning it into a quota</h2>
      <p>Repetition is one of those problems nobody notices until they suddenly do.</p>

      <p>Playing with the same partner twice can be perfectly fine. Playing six games and discovering that five involved the same three other people makes a large club feel surprisingly small.</p>

      <p>Variety should therefore be a preference, not usually an absolute prohibition. Sometimes the fairest next game contains a recent partner. Sometimes a league pair wants practice. Sometimes only five people are waiting and mathematics wins.</p>

      <p>A useful order of priorities for an ordinary social night is:</p>
      <ol>
        <li>do not create a serious court-time injustice;</li>
        <li>avoid an obviously poor game;</li>
        <li>then improve partner and opponent variety within the options left.</li>
      </ol>

      <p>That order prevents a clever rotation system from making somebody wait twenty-five minutes merely because every available partner has appeared in their history recently.</p>

      <h2 id="manual">A practical manual system</h2>
      <p>If you are running a board by hand, you can get surprisingly far with a few explicit rules.</p>

      <ol>
        <li><strong>Queue by genuine waiting.</strong> When players leave court, their waiting starts again.</li>
        <li><strong>Take the front player unless there is a good reason not to.</strong> This gives the queue an anchor everyone understands.</li>
        <li><strong>Choose the other three from a small window.</strong> Looking at the next six or eight rather than the entire room limits organiser bias.</li>
        <li><strong>Split obvious strength.</strong> Strong + developing versus strong + developing is usually preferable to strong + strong versus developing + developing.</li>
        <li><strong>Notice repeats.</strong> If the same partnership appears again, swap one player where that does not damage fairness.</li>
        <li><strong>Repay skips.</strong> If someone was passed over to create a viable game, put them at the front of the next suitable choice.</li>
      </ol>

      <p>This is not mathematically optimal. It is understandable, and understandability is part of fairness.</p>

      <h2 id="software">When software becomes useful</h2>
      <p>There is no player count at which a wooden board suddenly stops working.</p>

      <p>The trigger is <strong>state</strong>: how much information the club wants remembered when each court becomes free.</p>

      <p>If you only care about order, a board can handle a large room. If you care about actual wait, player level, recent partners, recent opponents, voluntary sit-outs, late arrivals and court-time distribution at once, somebody has to remember all of that.</p>

      <p><a href="/features/club-nights">ePegboard</a> is built for that version of the problem. It retains the queue and the visible "who is next?" part, while using session history and ratings when choosing the game. Clubs can also constrain the picker - for example by forcing the first waiting player into the next selection - rather than accepting a black box.</p>

      <p>The software should not define what your club considers fair. It should execute the definition more consistently than one tired organiser can at 9:45pm.</p>

      <div class="guide-callout">
        <h4>A fair rotation should be explainable</h4>
        <p style="margin-top:8px">If you cannot explain why Player A went on before Player B in one sentence, the rotation may be too opaque. "A had waited longer", "B asked to rest", or "B was skipped once so is next" are policies people can live with.</p>
      </div>

      <h2 id="faq">Frequently asked questions</h2>
      @foreach (var f in faqPairs)
      {
        <h3>@f.Q</h3>
        <p>@f.A</p>
      }

